.TH LFIND 8 2026-08-17 Lustre "Lustre Configuration Utilities"
.SH NAME
lfind \- scan a Lustre target on the server and list objects matching attributes
.SH SYNOPSIS
.SY lfind
.RI [ OPTION ]...
.RI [ FILTER ]...
.I TARGET
.YS
.SY lfind
.B --backend
.RB { ldiskfs | zfs | kmdt }
.RI [ OPTION ]...
.I TARGET
.YS
.SY lfind
.B --list-backends
.YS
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B lfind
reads the metadata of a single Lustre target \(em an MDT or an OST \(em on the
server that holds it, and writes one record per namespace-visible object to
standard output. It replaces a client-side
.BR lfs-find (1)
traversal for administrative queries over a whole target: nothing is walked
through the namespace, no RPCs are issued, and on an unmounted target no Lustre
kernel module need be loaded at all.
.PP
.B This is not
.BR lfs-find (1),
and the difference is not only one of speed. See
.B SEMANTICS
below before relying on a result, particularly for
.BR --size .
.SS Choosing a backend
Three backends read the three kinds of target. They are separate binaries
because a host with only
.B libext2fs
installed, or only
.BR libzpool ,
must still be able to build the one it can;
.B lfind
hides that by choosing from the target and executing the right one, passing
every other argument through unchanged.
.TP
.BR "a block device or a regular file" " \(em " lfind-ldiskfs
An unmounted ldiskfs MDT or OST, read
.B read-only
through libext2fs. A regular file is accepted because a loop-backed image is a
normal way to hold one.
.TP
.BI pool/dataset [ @snapshot ] " \(em " lfind-zfs
An unmounted ZFS target, or a snapshot of one, read through libzpool. Prefer a
snapshot: a live dataset cannot show the open transaction group.
.TP
.BR "a character device" " \(em " lfind-kmdt
A
.B mounted, serving
target, read through the
.B lfu_ring
kernel module, conventionally
.IR /dev/lfu_scan .
This is the only backend that works without taking the target offline, and the
only one where the filter is evaluated in the kernel.
.PP
The target is identified by scanning the arguments from the right for one that
is a device, an image or a stream, and only then for one merely shaped like a
dataset specification. A filter's value \(em
.BR +1G ,
.BR report* ,
.B fast
\(em is neither, and is skipped. Use
.B --backend
when a target is genuinely ambiguous, such as a
.B --name
pattern containing a slash that names no existing file.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Front-end options
.TP
.BR --backend " " { ldiskfs | zfs | kmdt }
Use this backend instead of choosing one from the target.
.TP
.B --list-backends
List the backends, and where each was found, then exit.
.SS Common options
.TP
.BR -j ", " --threads " " \fIN\fR
Run
.I N
workers over disjoint chunks of the object-id space. Record order is then
unspecified. Default 1. Not available on the
.B kmdt
backend, where the stream is a single reader; parallelism there belongs behind
the kernel enumerator.
.TP
.BR -i ", " --show-internal
Also emit internal objects, which are otherwise classified and skipped.
.TP
.BR -n ", " --limit " " \fIN\fR
Stop after
.I N
emitted records. Forces
.BR "-j 1" ,
since a limit is only well defined in one worker.
.TP
.BR -q ", " --quiet
Statistics only; no records.
.TP
.BR -u ", " --emit-unknown
Also emit records whose filter outcome is
.I unknown
(see
.BR SEMANTICS ),
tagged
.BR +unknown .
By default they are counted and not emitted.
.TP
.BR -v ", " --verbose
One class-tagged line per classified object, before filtering. A diagnostic, not
an output format.
.TP
.BR -h ", " --help
Usage, including the filter vocabulary, then exit.
.SS ZFS backend options
.TP
.BR -e ", " --import
Find the pool by scanning devices, as
.B zpool import
does. Required for Lustre pools, which are created with
.BR cachefile=none .
.TP
.BR -p ", " --search " " \fIDIR\fR
Directory to search for pool devices. Repeatable, default
.IR /dev ,
implies
.BR -e .
.TP
.B --force-active
Read a pool whose on-disk label is ACTIVE \(em imported elsewhere, or left dirty
by a crash. The read is still read-only, but only what reached disk is visible.
.SH FILTERS
The vocabulary is
.BR lfs-find (1)'s,
so a query written for one runs on the other.
.B !
before an option negates it,
.B +
means more than,
.B -
means less than, and a bare value must fall inside the unit it names \(em
.B --size 1M
is everything above zero up to 1 MiB. Several filters are ANDed.
.PP
Of
.BR lfs-find (1)'s
34 predicates, 33 are implemented; see
.B LIMITATIONS
for the exception.
.SS Attributes held in the inode or dnode
These cost nothing beyond the object's own record.
.TP
.BR --atime | --mtime | --ctime | --btime | --crtime " " [\fB+-\fR]\fIN\fR[\fBsmhdwy\fR]
Age of the object. Days if no unit is given. On an OST note that access time is
not maintained for objects, and precreated objects carry no time at all, so they
sort older than anything.
.TP
.BR --uid | --user " " \fIID\fR| \fINAME\fR, " " \fB--gid\fR| \fB--group\fR " " \fIID\fR| \fINAME\fR
Owner. A name is resolved through the local password or group database.
.TP
.BR --type " " { f | d | l | b | c | p | s }
Object type, as
.BR find (1)
spells it. The long names
.BR file ,
.BR dir ,
.BR link ,
.BR block ,
.BR char ,
.B pipe
and
.B sock
are accepted too.
.TP
.BR --perm " " [\fB/-\fR]\fIMODE\fR
Permission bits.
.I MODE
alone is exact,
.BI - MODE
requires all of those bits,
.BI / MODE
any of them.
.TP
.BR --links " " [\fB+-\fR]\fIN\fR
Link count.
.TP
.BI --projid " N"
Project ID.
.TP
.BR --attrs " " [\fB^\fR]\fIATTR\fR[\fB,\fR...]
Attribute flags, spelled as
.BR lfs-find (1)
spells them \(em either the letter or the long name:
.BR c " or " Compressed ,
.BR i " or " Immutable ,
.BR a " or " Append_Only ,
.BR d " or " No_Dump ,
.BR E " or " Encrypted .
.B ^
before one requires it absent, so
.B --attrs i,^E
selects immutable files that are not encrypted. Not every backend can see every
flag \(em see
.BR LIMITATIONS .
.TP
.BR --dev-blocks " " [\fB+-\fR]\fIN\fR
Blocks used by
.B this target's own
inode or dnode, compared in bytes with a bare number counting 512-byte units.
This is
.B not
the file's size on an MDT; it is the metadata object's own footprint. Provided
because it is occasionally what an administrator wants, and named so that it
cannot be mistaken for
.BR --blocks .
.SS Attributes held in an extended attribute
These are read only for objects that survive the filters above, and only the
attributes the query actually needs.
.TP
.BR --size | --blocks " " [\fB+-\fR]\fIN\fR[\fBbcKMGTPE\fR]
The
.B file's
size or block count, compared in bytes; a bare number counts 512-byte units.
On an MDT this is read from
.BR trusted.som ,
not from the inode \(em see
.BR SEMANTICS ,
which this option is the whole reason for.
.TP
.BR --stripe-count | --stripe-size | --mirror-count | --comp-count | --component-count " " [\fB+-\fR]\fIN\fR
Layout geometry, from
.BR trusted.lov .
.B --comp-count
counts composite (PFL) components;
.B --mirror-count
counts actual mirrors, so a non-FLR file is 1.
.TP
.BR --ost | --stripe-index " " \fIIDX\fR[\fB,\fIIDX\fR...][\fIN\fB-\fIM\fR]
Objects with a stripe on any of these OST indices. Ranges are accepted.
.B --stripe-index
is a spelling of
.BR --ost ,
not a separate predicate, matching
.BR lfs-find (1).
.TP
.BI --pool " NAME"
OST pool, from a v3 layout. Resolvable from the MDT alone; no OST scan is needed.
.TP
.BR --layout " " { released | raid0 | mdt | foreign | overstriped }[\fB,\fR...]
Layout pattern.
.B mdt
selects data-on-MDT components,
.B released
HSM-released files.
.TP
.BI --name " PATTERN"
Any of the object's names, from
.BR trusted.link .
.I PATTERN
is a shell glob. A hard-linked object matches on any of its links.
.TP
.BR --mdt-count " " [\fB+-\fR]\fIN\fR, " " \fB--mdt-hash\fR " " \fINAME\fR| \fIN\fR
Directory striping, from
.BR trusted.lmv .
.I NAME
is one of
.BR all_char ,
.BR fnv_1a_64 ,
.BR crush ,
.BR crush2 .
.SH SEMANTICS
.SS An MDT-only scan is lfs find --lazy
A striped regular file's data is on the OSTs, so its MDT inode's size is stale
or zero and its block count covers only the inode's own blocks. The single
number an MDT holds for the whole file is in the
.B trusted.som
extended attribute, written when the file is closed, and that is what
.B --size
and
.B --blocks
read.
.PP
This is exactly the
.B --lazy
mode of
.BR lfs-find (1),
which is a supported mode of that command and not an approximation. It has one
consequence worth stating plainly: a size or block filter has
.B three
outcomes, not two.
.TP
.B match
The size is known and satisfies the predicate.
.TP
.B no match
The size is known and does not.
.TP
.B unknown
The size is not known to this target: the object is a striped regular file with
no
.B trusted.som
yet, because it has not been closed since SOM was enabled. Such an object is
neither reported nor silently discarded \(em it is counted on its own line of the
summary, and
.B -u
emits it tagged
.BR +unknown .
.PP
A caller that must not miss an object cannot treat
.I unknown
as
.IR "no match" .
On a target where SOM is enabled and files are closed normally, the population is
usually zero, but it is never guaranteed to be.
.SS Metadata may be stale, and may be torn
On a mounted target the scan sees what has reached disk, which lags the live
state, and a structure may be read while it is being written. The scanners
validate what they read and count what they reject rather than interpreting it,
but a consumer that acts on a record should re-read the object first. The
.B kmdt
backend reads through the OSD and is not exposed to torn reads in the same way.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The scan completed.
.TP
.B 1
The target could not be opened or read, or the scan failed part way.
.TP
.B 2
The command line was wrong: an unparsable filter, no target, a target whose kind
could not be determined, or a filter this target cannot answer.
.TP
.B 127
The chosen backend is not built or not installed. Try
.BR --list-backends .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B LFIND_LIBEXEC
Directory to search for the backend binaries. The directory holding
.B lfind
itself is tried first, so a build tree works without installing.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Large files nobody has read for a month, on an unmounted MDT \(em two
inode-resident predicates and one extended attribute:
.RS
.EX
.B lfind --atime +30d --blocks +1G --projid 1999 /dev/sdb
.EE
.RE
.PP
The same query against a target that is mounted and serving, with the filter
evaluated in the kernel so that rejected objects never cross into user space:
.RS
.EX
.B modprobe lfu_ring dev=testfs-MDT0000-osd
.B lfind --atime +30d --blocks +1G /dev/lfu_scan
.EE
.RE
.PP
Everything in one OST pool, or on a particular OST, answered from the MDT alone:
.RS
.EX
.B lfind --pool fast /dev/sdb
.B lfind --ost 3,7 --stripe-count +1 /dev/sdb
.EE
.RE
.PP
Wide-striped files, and files whose layout keeps data on the MDT:
.RS
.EX
.B lfind --stripe-count +16 /dev/sdb
.B lfind --layout mdt /dev/sdb
.EE
.RE
.PP
A ZFS snapshot, importing the pool by scanning devices because a Lustre pool has
.BR cachefile=none :
.RS
.EX
.B lfind -e --type f --mtime +1y testpool/mdt0@backup
.EE
.RE
.PP
Count what a size filter cannot decide on this target, and then look at those
objects:
.RS
.EX
.B lfind -q --size +1G /dev/sdb
.B lfind -u --size +1G /dev/sdb | grep +unknown
.EE
.RE
.PP
Everything with two or more names, one of which looks like a report:
.RS
.EX
.B lfind --links +1 --name 'report*' /dev/sdb
.EE
.RE
.SH LIMITATIONS
.TP
.B Depth predicates are not implemented
.B --maxdepth
and
.B --mindepth
describe a position in a directory tree. This is a flat scan of a target's
objects and has no tree to be at a depth in; the two options are rejected rather
than ignored.
.TP
.B The kmdt backend needs a patched OSD
The
.B kmdt
backend reads extended attributes only through the OSD iterator. On an OSD
built without that support a filter needing
.BR trusted.som ,
.BR trusted.lov ,
.B trusted.lmv
or
.B trusted.link
is refused at start-up rather than answered from nothing; the message names the
attribute. Use
.B lfind-ldiskfs
or
.B lfind-zfs
against an unmounted target or a snapshot instead.
.TP
.B --attrs sees fewer flags on ZFS
.B z_pflags
has no per-file compressed or encrypted bit \(em both are dataset properties \(em
so those two names are refused on a ZFS target rather than reported as absent.
.TP
.B No aggregation
.B lfind
selects; it does not summarise. There is no
.BR count ,
.BR sum ,
.B histogram
or
.BR "largest N" .
.TP
.B One target per invocation
Merging the streams of several targets, and removing the duplicate objects that
LMR mirroring produces across MDTs, is not done here.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /dev/lfu_scan
The object stream exported by the
.B lfu_ring
module for a mounted target.
.TP
.I /usr/sbin/lfind
.TQ
.IR /usr/sbin/lfind- { ldiskfs | zfs | kmdt }
The front-end and the backends are installed in the same directory, so the
front-end finds a backend beside itself without a compiled-in path. Expect only
the backends this host could build to be present;
.B --list-backends
reports which those are.
.SH NOTES
Reading a block device directly exposes every object's metadata on the target
with no access control of any kind, and the names in
.B trusted.link
are user data. This is an administrator's tool: run it as a user in the device's
group rather than as root where you can, and treat its output as being as
sensitive as the namespace itself.
.PP
Do not confuse this command with
.BR lfind (3),
an unrelated C library function for linear search in an array.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR lfs-find (1),
.BR lfs (1),
.BR lctl (8),
.BR lfsck (8),
.BR mount.lustre (8),
.BR find (1),
.BR lfind (3)
